// Practice quiz: Spring vs Spring Boot: what Boot adds
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-boot-basics/spring-vs-spring-boot-what-boot-adds

::o1 framework role::Which part of a Spring application still comes from Spring Framework, not Spring Boot? {
~The curated dependency versions#Not quite. Those come from the Boot BOM.
=The container that creates and injects beans#Correct. Spring Boot starts the container but does not implement it.
~The auto-configuration conditions#Not quite. Those are a Boot feature.
~The starter dependency sets#Not quite. Starters are Boot artifacts.
}

::o1 what Boot is not::A student says Boot replaces Spring MVC. What is the accurate correction? {
~Boot replaces it with a faster framework#Not quite. Boot does not bring a different MVC framework for this stack.
=Boot configures Spring MVC, and controllers still use it#Correct. Controllers keep using Spring MVC annotations and behavior.
~Boot only applies to database code#Not quite. Boot applies across web, data and more.
~Boot removes the need for controllers#Not quite. You still write controllers.
}

::o2 starters::What is a Spring Boot starter? {
~A script that starts the application#Not quite. That is the run command or Maven plugin.
~A required base class for your code#Not quite. Starters are dependencies, not classes you extend.
=A convenient set of managed dependencies#Correct. A starter bundles what a capability needs, with versions Boot manages.
~An installer for an application server#Not quite. No server install is involved.
}

::o2 version management::You declare a managed dependency without a version in a project that uses the Boot parent. Which version do you get? {
~The newest version published anywhere#Not quite. Boot does not float to the newest version.
~No version, so the build fails#Not quite. That is what happens without dependency management.
~The version of the JDK#Not quite. JDK and library versions are unrelated.
=The version curated by Boot dependency management#Correct. The BOM supplies a tested version.
}

::o3 executable jar::Why does java -jar start a web server for a packaged Boot application? {
=The executable jar contains the embedded server#Correct. The Boot Maven plugin repackages the classes, dependencies and server together.
~Java includes a web server for every jar#Not quite. A plain jar has no web server.
~The command installs Tomcat first#Not quite. Nothing is installed; it is already inside the jar.
~Maven stays running in the background#Not quite. Maven is not needed once the jar exists.
}

